  "context": "Origin: 2026-11-03 root-cause analysis of TPC-H RF1 timing (5 whys) and its adversarial review. Spec clauses cited here were read from the published PDFs at the links on 2026-11-03; re-read them rather than trusting this text.\nThis page is the single place later timer changes cite. Every item that moves a timer boundary must update its row in the same PR.\n\n2026-11-03 correction round: ADR and result-schema item added as recorded decisions to cite; 5.3.7.2 paraphrase corrected; deviations list extended; acceptance made call-site based; unrelated link removed. Code items update their rows conditionally, so this item is not a prerequisite for them.\n\nRow text from batch tpch-refresh-path (docs/internal/tpc-measured-intervals.md absent on base):\n- TPC-H throughput refresh stream: window starts after the refresh connection factory returns (start_monotonic, start_time) and ends before the connection closes (end_monotonic, end_time); connection setup excluded; these bounds feed the Ts span in StreamRunner.compute_metrics.\n- TPC-H RF1: interval = BEGIN, reading and parsing orders.tbl.uN / lineitem.tbl.uN, the insert (native reader on DuckDB: INSERT ... SELECT FROM read_csv; PostgreSQL: COPY FROM STDIN; batched INSERT ... VALUES elsewhere) and COMMIT; file generation is outside it. DuckLake and SQLite stay on VALUES.\n- SingleStore RF1: batched INSERT ... VALUES inside the RF1 timer (no native ingest); LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE availability and parity not verified (no container runtime on the host).\n\n2026-11-05 (final PR #3960): page written at docs/internal/tpc-measured-intervals.md (28 rows, findings F1-F8 with no tracker owner listed there, #3948 changes listed as pending). timing_policy_check.py extension: not pursued; the checker already reports about 197 informational unknown findings, so a duration-pair rule would add noise. Candidate new items for F1-F8 (TPC-DS throughput generation inside the timer when enable_preflight is false, legacy TPCDSBenchmark.run_power_test timing get_query, TPC-DI source generation inside timed phases) are left for the owner to file.",
  "description": "Problem. Which work sits inside each benchmark timer is decided by spec clauses, but no repo page cites them. The TPC-H RF1 cost analysis missed clause 5.3.7.10 for that reason, and four tracker items carry timer claims that the specs contradict or confirm without anyone having checked. The root cause recorded in the analysis is that the TPC timing clauses are not captured in the repo as cited, tested requirements.\n\nDeliverable: docs/internal/tpc-measured-intervals.md (unpublished page; do not add it to docs/publish-allowlist.txt). One row per timer site in benchbox/core/tpch, tpcds, tpcdi and throughput that starts or stops a measured interval: TPC-H and TPC-DS per-query power timing, RF1 and RF2, the refresh stream window, throughput stream windows and Ts, TPC-DS data maintenance, TPC-DI load and ETL. Columns: file and symbol (no bare line numbers), governing clause (spec, version, number), what is inside the interval, what is outside, deviation status (none, or the tracker item that owns the fix). Link it from docs/internal/throughput-result-alignment.md (timing-window paragraph).\n\nRecorded decision to reconcile and cite: the accepted locality ADR amendment 'Round Trip Removed and Platform Timings' (item 6: timers measure only the query; adapters move tag setup, stats lookups, validation, plan capture and power-test query generation out of the measured timer; runs record run.timing_method and are compared and ranked within one method). The ADR moved to the site repository in #3545; its pre-move text is docs/development/adr/adr-client-link-locality-disclosure.md at commit e4a8a09091^. The open item result-schema-2-4-producer-20261022 owns writing timing_method (the code reads it but no producer writes it). Cite both in the page.\n\nClauses to verify and cite (read each from the spec, then paraphrase; quote at most one sentence per clause):\n- TPC-H 3.0.1 (https://www.tpc.org/tpc_documents_current_versions/pdf/tpc-h_v3.0.1.pdf): 2.5.3.1, 5.3.6.1 (Ts), 5.3.7.2 (a query interval runs from submission of the query to submission of the next query, or for the last query to the last output character received by the driver; list BenchBox's interval endpoint as a deviation if it differs), 5.3.7.3 (RF interval: first character submitted until last transaction committed), 5.3.7.10 (before the refresh stream the dbgen data may only be generated, permuted and relocated; formatting and database activity are timed).\n- TPC-DS 4.0.0 (https://www.tpc.org/tpc_documents_current_versions/pdf/tpc-ds_v4.0.0.pdf): 5.2.3 (dsdgen execution not timed), 5.2.5 and 5.2.7 (loading the refresh data, including into a staging area, is timed), 7.4.9.1 (maintenance interval start).\n- TPC-DI 1.1.0 (https://www.tpc.org/tpc_documents_current_versions/pdf/tpc-di_v1.1.0.pdf): find and cite the clause numbers for what is timed (transformation begins with the SUT reading Source Data) and what is untimed (data generation, copy to staging). No clause number for TPC-DI was verified when this item was written.\n\nKnown deviations to list, each linked to its item: power-test-times-query-generation-20261031; result-schema-2-4-producer-20261022 (row-count validation and metadata propagation inside the power per-query timer); derived-power-export-rounding-20261017; perf-tpch-rf1-bulk-load-20261031 (cost, not a deviation); tpch-refresh-precheck-timing-decision-20261103 (three sites, including the standalone maintenance test); tpch-refresh-stream-window-excludes-connect-20261103; tpcds-maintenance-timer-hygiene-20261103; tpcds-maintenance-data-source-decision-20261103 and tpcds-maintenance-dsdgen-refresh-data-20261103; fetch-timing-contract-decision-20261103. Also check, and list if confirmed, whether TPC-DS throughput query generation runs inside the timer when enable_preflight is False (benchbox/core/tpcds/throughput_test.py). List the deviations recorded in the tracker item tpch-refresh-functions-in-power-and-throughput (duration rounding, 1000x clamp, Ts rounding, two-run rule) by reading it.\n\nGuardrails for all code work: work only in a linked worktree (make worktree-create); never edit the primary clone. No explanatory comments or docstrings in source (make comment-policy-check). Durations use benchbox.utils.clock.mono_time()/elapsed_seconds(). Add a changelog.d/ fragment. Cited line numbers are orientation only: re-read the named symbols, docs and spec clauses before editing and treat a mismatch as a reason to stop and update this item. No live-cloud runs without owner approval; heavy runs only through make heavy-run or a lock-taking entry point. Green focused tests are not release certification.",
